1.2 Regulators
Drug development is a highly regulated process, given the requirement to experiment on humans, and the implications for treatment of patients in the future. Regulatory bodies include

The Medicines & Healthcare products Regulatory Agency (MHRA) in the UK;
The European Medicines Agency (EMA) in the European Union and in particular the Committee for Medicinal Products for Human Use (CHMP);
The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) in the USA;
The National Medical Products Administration in China.

Regulators may produce guidelines related to various issues regarding the design, conduct and analysis of clinical trials, but in some cases will adopt guidelines produced by International Council for Harmonisation of Technical Requirements for Pharmaceuticals for Human Use (ICH). Of particular relevance to this module is

E9: Statistical Principles for Clinical Trials

In the UK, even after a drug is licensed, it may not be available to NHS patients. Here, there is another regulatory body, the National Institute of Health and Care Excellence (NICE), that makes recommendations on the availability of treatments based on their cost-effectiveness. The NHS Constitution for England states that “You have the right to drugs and treatments that have been recommended by NICE for use in the NHS, if your doctor says they are clinically appropriate for you.”
There are registries of clinical trials, e.g. the International Clinical Trials Registry Platform managed by the World Health Organisation. In the UK, it is a legal requirement to register a trial and publish summary results on such a registry.

8.2 Binary data
Here, we again consider a simplified analysis, where we do not consider any covariates. We suppose that

the number of ‘successes’ \(X_c\) in the control group has the \(Binomial(n, \theta_c)\) distribution;
the number of ‘successes’ \(X_t\) in the treatment group has the \(Binomial(n,\theta_t)\) distribution;
once the data are obtained, we test the hypothesis \(H_0:\theta_c = \theta_t\), using a two sample \(Z\)-test, assuming the sample proportions \(X_c/n\) and \(X_t/n\) are normally distributed.

We follow the same approach before of specifying the desired power of the test and deriving the required sample size. Two differences are

we first specify \(\theta_c\), for example using historical data, and then set \(\theta_t = \theta_c+\delta\), where \(\delta\) is the minimum clinically important difference;
there is no separate variance parameter to estimate; variances are determined once \(\theta_c,\theta_t\) and \(n\) are specified.

We won’t work through the derivation here, but it is similar to the normally distributed case above. We obtain
\[
n =\frac{\left(\Phi^{-1}(\alpha/2)+\Phi^{-1}(\beta)\right)^2(\theta_c(1-\theta_c)+ \theta_t(1-\theta_t))}{\delta^2}
\] As before, we might inflate this number if we expect patients to drop out of the trial.
